// expect-exit 2
//
// The other half of tests/std/test_collections.orb: a search over a list
// whose elements are lists. `list_of_lists` hands the list back behind a
// bare `-> list`, so the front end cannot see what is in there and E1005
// cannot fire; passing it into `indexOfStr(l: list<string>, ...)` checks
// nothing by itself -- measured: the same value bound to a `list<string>`
// parameter that never reads exits 0. The refusal comes at the first typed
// read inside the search, `l.get(i)`, which lowers to
// `orbit_list_read_string` and its read prologue `orbit_list_checked` ->
// `orbit_list_expect_kind` (runtime/orbit_api.h). The list holds a list --
// kind `reference` -- and the read wants `string`, so
// `orbit_list_kind_error` (runtime/collections.c) stops the program:
//
//     $ orbit run tests/std/test_collections_boxed.orb
//     orbit: list element type mismatch
//       list holds reference, this value is string
//       a list has one element type, so a read and a write must agree on it
//
// Exit 2 is what the refusal exits with, and it is the assertion: refusing
// is the answer the contract wants, because a string search over a list of
// lists has no coherent answer, and answering anyway is the F-0009 shape --
// the read compared a list pointer with the needle and returned -1. The
// static half of the same rule, where the element type is nameable at the
// call site, is E1005:
//
//     error[E1005]: Parameter 1 of indexOfStr is declared list<string> but
//     the argument is list<list<string>>.
//
// and the push-side half is tests/negative/list_mixed_element_kinds.orb.
// The two checks below are unreachable while the refusal holds. They stay
// so that a tree where it stops happening is judged by what the searches
// answer instead.
//
// This file carried `known-failing: F-0002` until the ratchet was
// re-measured, and the premise was wrong twice over: F-0002 and F-0009 are
// both fixed, and F-0009's fix IS this refusal -- giving lists an element
// type did not make this search answer 0, it made the incoherent read stop.
// The runner counts a non-zero exit as "still red as declared", so the
// declaration could never flip: the file had already stopped failing the
// way it claimed. Ordinary expected-exit coverage is what it wanted to be.
import "std/collections/lists.orb"
import "std/test/assert.orb"

fn main() -> int {
    val outer = list_of_lists()
    if assertEqInt(indexOfStr(outer, "x"), 0) == false { return 1 }
    if assertTrue(containsStr(outer, "x")) == false { return 2 }
    return 0
}

fn list_of_lists() -> list {
    var inner = []
    inner.push("x")
    var outer = []
    outer.push(inner)
    return outer
}
